; seed.t27 — Minimal Golden Seed for E2E CI (#150)
; φ² + 1/φ² = 3 | TRINITY

module seed;

// ============================================================================
// Constants - Trit Values (Balanced Ternary)
// ============================================================================

pub const NEGONE : i8 = -1;
pub const ZERO : i8 = 0;
pub const ONE : i8 = 1;

// Trit enum for type safety
pub const Trit = enum(i8) {
    neg = -1,
    zero = 0,
    pos = 1,
};

// ============================================================================
// Functions
// ============================================================================

// trit_negate(a: Trit) -> Trit
// Negate a trit: -1 -> +1, 0 -> 0, +1 -> -1
pub fn trit_negate(a: Trit) Trit {
    return switch (a) {
        .neg => .pos,
        .zero => .zero,
        .pos => .neg,
    };
}

// trit_add(a: Trit, b: Trit) -> Trit
// Balanced ternary addition
pub fn trit_add(a: Trit, b: Trit) Trit {
    return switch (a) {
        .neg => switch (b) {
            .neg => .neg,
            .zero => .neg,
            .pos => .zero,
        },
        .zero => b,
        .pos => switch (b) {
            .neg => .zero,
            .zero => .pos,
            .pos => .pos,
        },
    };
}

// ============================================================================
// TDD - Tests
// ============================================================================

test "test_trit_negate_neg_to_pos" {
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Trit, .pos), trit_negate(.neg));
}

test "test_trit_negate_zero_to_zero" {
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Trit, .zero), trit_negate(.zero));
}

test "test_trit_negate_pos_to_neg" {
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Trit, .neg), trit_negate(.pos));
}

test "test_trit_add_neg_plus_pos_equals_zero" {
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Trit, .zero), trit_add(.neg, .pos));
}

test "test_trit_add_identity" {
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Trit, .neg), trit_add(.zero, .neg));
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Trit, .zero), trit_add(.zero, .zero));
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(Trit, .pos), trit_add(.zero, .pos));
}

// ============================================================================
// TDD - Invariants
// ============================================================================

invariant trit_value_range {
    @compileAssert(@intFromEnum(Trit.neg) == -1);
    @compileAssert(@intFromEnum(Trit.zero) == 0);
    @compileAssert(@intFromEnum(Trit.pos) == 1);
}

invariant trit_negate_involutive {
    @compileAssert(true);
}

// ============================================================================
// Article Content
// ============================================================================

// The approved article text for "the-seed-moved-it-the-pins-moved-it-more"
pub const ARTICLE_TEXT: str = "The Seed Moved It: The Pins Moved It More

In the intricate landscape of computational systems, there exists a fundamental principle that governs how information propagates and transforms. This principle, often overlooked in mainstream discourse, reveals itself through the subtle interplay between seeds and pins—two seemingly disparate concepts that, when understood in their proper context, illuminate the very nature of system evolution.

The seed represents the initial state, the foundational configuration from which all subsequent developments emerge. It is the compact encoding of potential, the latent blueprint that contains within it the entire trajectory of what is to come. In computational terms, the seed is the starting point, the input that sets the entire process in motion.

What makes the seed particularly fascinating is its role as both cause and effect. While it initiates the sequence of events, it is also shaped by the constraints and conditions that surround it. The seed does not exist in isolation; it is embedded within a larger ecosystem that defines its possibilities and limitations.

This is where the pins come into play. Pins are the constraints, the fixed points that guide and direct the evolution of the system. They are the reference points against which all movement is measured, the anchors that give meaning to transformation. Without pins, movement would be meaningless—there would be no way to distinguish change from stasis, evolution from stagnation.

The relationship between seeds and pins is not hierarchical but complementary. The seed provides the impetus for movement, while the pins provide the framework within which that movement occurs. Together, they create a dynamic tension that drives the system forward while maintaining coherence and purpose.

What we observe in complex systems is that when the seed moves—when the initial configuration shifts—the entire system responds. But the nature and extent of this response is determined by the pins. Some pins are rigid and unyielding, creating boundaries that cannot be crossed. Others are flexible and adaptive, allowing for greater freedom of movement.

The truly remarkable aspect of this relationship is that the pins themselves can move. When the pins shift—when the constraints change—the very nature of what is possible shifts with them. This creates a recursive relationship where movement begets movement, and change begets further change.

In practical terms, this understanding has profound implications for how we design and interact with complex systems. Whether we are building software architectures, managing organizational change, or understanding natural phenomena, the interplay between seeds and pins provides a powerful framework for predicting and influencing outcomes.

The seed moved it—yes, but the pins moved it more. It is in the careful calibration of both that we find the key to understanding and mastering the complex systems that surround us.";
